        link "View on GitHub" https://github.com/twbs/bootstrap/blob/v5.3.8/site/src/content/docs/getting-started/parcel.mdx
        h1 Bootstrap and Parcel
      text The official guide for how to include and bundle Bootstrap’s CSS and JavaScript in your project using Parcel.
    col
      text On this page
      link "What is Parcel?" #what-is-parcel
      link Setup #setup
      link "Project structure" #project-structure
      link "Configure Parcel" #configure-parcel
      link "Import Bootstrap" #import-bootstrap
      card
        link "https://srv.carbonads.net/ads/click/x/GTND4277CVBDV2JJCYS4YKQUF67ITKQWCV7I5Z3JCAAI627ICEBDC5QKCK7IE5QUC6YI55QLFTBIE5Q7CVSI4KJKC6SILKJWCA7DVK3EHJNCLSIZ" https://srv.carbonads.net/ads/click/x/GTND4277CVBDV2JJCYS4YKQUF67ITKQWCV7I5Z3JCAAI627ICEBDC5QKCK7IE5QUC6YI55QLFTBIE5Q7CVSI4KJKC6SILKJWCA7DVK3EHJNCLSIZ
        link "AWS Training & Certification Roles change. AWS credentials travel with you." https://srv.carbonads.net/ads/click/x/GTND4277CVBDV2JJCYS4YKQUF67ITKQWCV7I5Z3JCAAI627ICEBDC5QKCK7IE5QUC6YI55QLFTBIE5Q7CVSI4KJKC6SILKJWCA7DVK3EHJNCLSIZ
      link "ads via Carbon" http://carbonads.net/?utm_source=getbootstrapcom&utm_medium=ad_via_link&utm_campaign=in_unit&utm_term=carbon
    col
      text Download the source code and working demo for this guide from the . You can also  but not run it because Parcel isn’t currently supported there. primary
      text Want to skip to the end? primary
      link "twbs/examples repository" https://github.com/twbs/examples/tree/main/parcel
      link "open the example in StackBlitz" https://stackblitz.com/github/twbs/examples/tree/main/parcel?file=index.html
      h2 What is Parcel?
      text is a web application bundler designed to simplify the development process with a zero-configuration setup out of the box. It offers features found in more advanced bundlers while focusing on ease of use, making it ideal for developers seeking a quick start.
      link Parcel https://parceljs.org/
      h2 Setup
      text We’re building a Parcel project with Bootstrap from scratch, so there are some prerequisites and upfront steps before we can really get started. This guide requires you to have Node.js installed and some familiarity with the terminal.
      text Create a project folder and set up npm. We'll create the my-project folder and initialize npm with the -y argument to avoid it asking us all the interactive questions.
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text mkdir my-project && cd my-project npm init -y
      text Install Parcel. Unlike our Webpack guide, there’s only a single build tool dependency here. Parcel will automatically install language transformers (like Sass) as it detects them. We use --save-dev to signal that this dependency is only for development use and not for production.
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text npm i --save-dev parcel
      text Install Bootstrap. Now we can install Bootstrap. We'll also install Popper since our dropdowns, popovers, and tooltips depend on it for their positioning. If you don’t plan on using those components, you can omit Popper here.
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text npm i --save bootstrap @popperjs/core
      text Now that we have all the necessary dependencies installed, we can get to work creating the project files and importing Bootstrap.
      h2 Project structure
      text We’ve already created the my-project folder and initialized npm. Now we'll also create our src folder, stylesheet, and JavaScript file to round out the project structure. Run the following from my-project, or manually create the folder and file structure shown below.
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text mkdir {src,src/js,src/scss} touch src/index.html src/js/main.js src/scss/styles.scss
      text When you’re done, your complete project should look like this:
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text my-project/ ├── src/ │ ├── js/ │ │ └── main.js │ ├── scss/ │ │ └── styles.scss │ └── index.html ├── package-lock.json └── package.json
      text At this point, everything is in the right place, but Parcel needs an HTML page and npm script to start our server.
      h2 Configure Parcel
      text With dependencies installed and our project folder ready for us to start coding, we can now configure Parcel and run our project locally. Parcel itself requires no configuration file by design, but we do need an npm script and an HTML file to start our server.
      text Fill in the src/index.html file. Parcel needs a page to render, so we use our index.html page to set up some basic HTML, including our CSS and JavaScript files.
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text "<!doctype html> <html lang=\"en\"> <head> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"> <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"> <title>Bootstrap w/ Parcel</title> <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"scss/styles.scss\"> <script type=\"module\" src=\"js/main.js\"></script> </head> <body> <div class=\"container py-4 px-3 mx-auto\"> <h1>Hello, Bootstrap and Parcel!</h1> <button class=\"btn btn-primary\">Primary button</button> </div> </body> </html>"
      text "We’re including a little bit of Bootstrap styling here with the div class=\"container\" and <button> so that we see when Bootstrap’s CSS is loaded by Parcel."
      text Parcel will automatically detect we’re using Sass and install the  to support it. However, if you wish, you can also manually run .
      link "Sass Parcel plugin" https://parceljs.org/languages/sass/
      text npm i --save-dev @parcel/transformer-sass danger
      text Add the Parcel npm scripts. Open the package.json and add the following start script to the scripts object. We'll use this script to start our Parcel development server and render the HTML file we created after it’s compiled into the dist directory.
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text "{ // ... \"scripts\": { \"start\": \"parcel serve src/index.html --public-url / --dist-dir dist\", \"test\": \"echo \\\"Error: no test specified\\\" && exit 1\" }, // ... }"
      text And finally, we can start Parcel. From the my-project folder in your terminal, run that newly added npm script:
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text npm start
      image https://getbootstrap.com/docs/5.3/assets/img/guides/parcel-dev-server.png "Parcel dev server running"
      text In the next and final section to this guide, we'll import all of Bootstrap’s CSS and JavaScript.
      h2 Import Bootstrap
      text Importing Bootstrap into Parcel requires two imports, one into our styles.scss and one into our main.js.
      text Import Bootstrap’s CSS. Add the following to src/scss/styles.scss to import all of Bootstrap’s source Sass.
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text "// Import all of Bootstrap’s CSS @import \"bootstrap/scss/bootstrap\";"
      text You can also import our stylesheets individually if you want.  for details.
      link "Read our Sass import docs" /docs/5.3/customize/sass#importing
      text Optional: You may see Sass deprecation warnings with the latest versions of Dart Sass. These can silenced by adding the following configuration in a .sassrc.js file in the root folder with the following:
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text module.exports = { silenceDeprecations: ['import', 'mixed-decls', 'color-functions', 'global-builtin'] }
      text Import Bootstrap’s JS. Add the following to src/js/main.js to import all of Bootstrap’s JS. Popper will be imported automatically through Bootstrap.
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text // Import all of Bootstrap’s JS import * as bootstrap from 'bootstrap'
      text You can also import JavaScript plugins individually as needed to keep bundle sizes down:
      card
        button Copy
        card
          text import Alert from 'bootstrap/js/dist/alert' // or, specify which plugins you need: import { Tooltip, Toast, Popover } from 'bootstrap'
      text for more information on how to use Bootstrap’s plugins.
      link "Read our JavaScript docs" /docs/5.3/getting-started/javascript/
      text And you’re done! 🎉 With Bootstrap’s source Sass and JS fully loaded, your local development server should now look like this:
      image https://getbootstrap.com/docs/5.3/assets/img/guides/parcel-dev-server-bootstrap.png "Parcel dev server running with Bootstrap"
      text Now you can start adding any Bootstrap components you want to use. Be sure to  for how to include additional custom Sass and optimize your build by importing only the parts of Bootstrap’s CSS and JS that you need.
      link "check out the complete Parcel example project" https://github.com/twbs/examples/tree/main/parcel
      text See something wrong or out of date here? Please . Need help troubleshooting?  on GitHub.
      link "open an issue on GitHub" https://github.com/twbs/bootstrap/issues/new/choose
      link "Search or start a discussion" https://github.com/twbs/bootstrap/discussions
